diagnosis:
Producers do not export what consumers need. The boundary 'no record type standing in for an object' (`cluster/cdk8s/AGENTS.md:8-13`) left a bare string constant as the only sanctioned carrier for a fact that crosses modules. Results:
- one port is written 6 times in one module (`ntfy.py:56` defines it; 190, 218, 222, 235-236 and 253 reuse it);
- `<cluster>-app` and `<name>-rw.<ns>.svc` are derived by hand 12 times (`terraform.py:32`, `atuin/user_provisioner.py:36-37`);
- the `external-creds-reader` literal appears 31 times;
- a consumer re-declares a producer's bearer name and key (`agentplane/staging.py:382, 389` against `ha_mcp.py:66-67`);
- `single_secret_store` returns a `str` (`external_secrets/single_secret_store.py:11-19`).
Only `Service(selector=deployment)` and Settings-derived env names hold by construction.

Cross-cutting pod policy reaches every dialect but is applied to one. `pod_policy.harden()` and `place()` find a workload's pod spec through `POD_SPEC_PATHS` (`pod_policy.py:19-30`), the table the fleet rules also read, yet all 20 `harden()` and 8 `place()` calls are on cdk8s-plus workloads; the 54 raw `k8s.Kube*` workloads, `SandboxTemplate` and `ScaledJob` get neither. Hardening is carried three ways:
- constructor keywords;
- `harden()`;
- literal structs (`nix_cache/attic.py:263-276`, `agent_workspaces.py:75-97`).
Placement facts are each named once in `node_scheduling.py`, and a placement renders one way, as a `nodeSelector`: `place()` writes it on its 8 cdk8s-plus workloads, while the raw pod specs, CNPG clusters, Seaweed specs and Helm values state `HIL_OVH_NODE_SELECTOR` by hand.

Enforcement depends on the author remembering. Fleet rules are registered per chart by an explicit `add_fleet_rules` call (`fleet_rules.py:143-144`) on 12 of 190 charts. Node facts are restated at each node:
- `SOPS_DECRYPTION` is passed by hand at 22 nodes, against 26 that derive it from `write_directory`'s SOPS siblings and 1 `sops_decryption` call;
- 39 health-check entries are typed out (for example `dns_automation.py:87-94`), against 1 `flux.health_checks` call site, which feeds 2 nodes;
- 21 of 40 stateful Kustomizations lack the Orphan that `cluster/cdk8s/AGENTS.md:16-20` requires.
The graph check has the same gap: it reads `kustomize build` output, so nothing checks the 11 CRD edges known only from their comments, mostly for kinds a Helm chart renders, and `parse_cluster` reads Flux Kustomizations only from `kustomizations.k8s.yaml` (`cluster/validation/cluster.py:144-150`), so `haku-state-workloads`, which `haku-workloads.k8s.yaml` renders, is not a graph node at all.

Authorization is kept in sync by hand. Copying one Secret between namespaces has at least five mechanisms:
- the external-creds roster;
- `single_secret_store` per-pair stores built from the consumer's chart;
- app-owned ESO-ServiceAccount stores (`ntfy.py:70-82`);
- reflector mirrors;
- ClusterExternalSecret.
Store namespace allowlists are hand-kept copies of the consumer list (the outages are recorded in `TODO.md`; `external_secrets/config.py:68-91`). The committed YAML has three sources for the same `flux-system/external-creds-reader` ServiceAccount: two live Kustomizations (generated/dns-automation, k8s/github-secrets-sync-secrets) and the parked `k8s/parked/cloud-agent-tf`.

The Flux graph was built for readiness, so it grew tiny units and `wait` everywhere; its edges are mostly admission edges. On devel there are 169 Kustomizations and 241 `dependsOn` edges:
- 216 are admission edges: 186 to a CRD provider (11 of them known only from the comment beside the edge, mostly for kinds a Helm chart renders) and 30 to a `failurePolicy: Fail` webhook, 27 of them direct `kyverno` edges, which decision 2 would reclassify as runtime; none is to a `postBuild` source;
- the other 25 wait on runtime state: 17 on namespace units, all of which #8185 removes, 5 carry a #7094 rule-2 reason (`bootstrap-never-converges`, `destructive-if-out-of-order`), 2 order a user-provisioner Job after its app, and `artifact-generators` waits on `flux-system`;
- `wait` is true on 121 nodes, and 92 of them are unsuspended and gate nothing admission needs;
- 55 of 163 measurable units render 3 objects or fewer.
The owner is moving the graph to admission-only edges (#7094) because the tiny units cost reconcile time and I/O, but the cdk8s layer still defaults `wait=True` (`flux.py:142`) and nothing checks that an edge is justified: a rule-2 reason is a comment.

one_service:
`ntfy.py` (294 lines) is a typical small HTTP service. It uses:
- construct classes: `Deployment`, `Service`, `ServiceMonitor`, `ExternalSecret`;
- resource functions: `https_route`, `cnpg.cluster`, `kubernetes_store.cluster_secret_store`;
- a mutator that writes a JSON patch: `pod_policy.harden`;
- value factories: `http_probe`, `cnpg.same_owner_initdb`, and a module-local `_secret_env`;
- an opt-in validation: `fleet_rules.add_fleet_rules(chart)`;
- a Flux node that takes the `RenderedDirectory` the entry point's `write_directory` call returns (`ntfy.py:273-294`, `generate_manifests.py:753-761`).
The `PORT` constant is threaded by hand into 6 places, `_LABELS` into 6, and the UID `65532` is written 4 times. The route names its backend as the string `NAME`, not the Service it builds.

conventions.adoption:
On devel, 44 of the 46 provider kind constructors take `metadata=`, every variant class returns the generated struct, and no CRD kind is built raw at more than one site. SeaweedFS's `Identity` and `Bucket` (`providers/seaweedfs/s3.py:59-120`) still take `name=, namespace=`, and only their own test calls them (step 5).
Five shapes stay as they are until a second caller needs one changed:
- the ExternalSecret `target` stays flattened (`creation_policy` at 35 of 39 sites);
- `redis_operator` is heavily flattened for its one site;
- kyverno's `match_resources` is module-level;
- `ActionPolicySet` and flux's `Kustomization` are each built at one site, inside a function with many callers;
- the names: cilium's `NetworkPolicy` against `CiliumNetworkPolicy`, and the `flux_` prefix in `flux_kustomization.py`.

